**Read:** [Engineering Geology Field Manual — U.S. Bureau of Reclamation](../sources/USBR-Engineering-Geology-Field-Manual.lingua#r_5WS5PNK19008B3SV6D1WWNVTRE), the complete groundwater, permeability-test, water-control, and documentation methods; [Water stewardship — Australian leading-practice handbook](../sources/Mine-Water-Stewardship-Handbook.lingua#r_4Y8Q03RFJBVBN6WVVY3EN6Q5DM), the full handbook; [Hard Rock Miner’s Handbook — Jack de la Vergne, fifth edition](../sources/Hard-Rock-Miners-Handbook.lingua#r_18YBY5XX4TFS7ZQSKTQMW34EFA), the complete pumping/dewatering and service context; [Derive a groundwater-flow account from head, properties, and boundaries](../../../../nature/earth/geology/water/Groundwater-head-conductivity-and-flow.lingua#r_104ZRDJ685JFSD9E3RDJVN8NQB), the full shared head, conductivity, and flow explanation.

Dewatering changes groundwater conditions and interacts with excavation, structures, recharge, surrounding water users/environments, ground behavior, and closure. A pump duty is one component of a hydrogeological system whose boundaries and properties can change through time.

Separate observed water levels/flows, the conceptual model, calculated drawdown/inflow, equipment performance, and consequences. Fractures, heterogeneity, storage, delayed drainage, surface connections, and evolving workings can make a simple uniform steady-flow model inadequate.

For invented water density 1,000 kg m⁻³, flow 0.02 m³ s⁻¹, total head 100 m, g = 9.81 m s⁻², and assumed efficiency 0.7, P = ρgQH/η gives about 28.029 kW. This is a hydraulic energy account, not a complete pump/system selection or an established mine inflow prediction.

**Work:** derive/check the example and reconstruct a full dewatering case with field methods, conceptual model, pump/system observations, water destinations, uncertainty, and response monitoring. Compare a plausible boundary/connectivity interpretation and identify a measurement capable of distinguishing it.

Read full groundwater-data and water-stewardship methods plus the relevant handbook chapters. The next water-balance step connects mine inflow/outflow with the processing circuit, catchment, inventories, and lifecycle rather than treating pumped water as an isolated waste stream.

